{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c731","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0761f-a1d7-7f21-bbcd-d353d9bc9dd9"},"sequence":499,"recorded_at":1780531400000993,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"357b4990-0035-4254-a95a-ceb9ed1d0622","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"357b4990-0035-4254-a95a-ceb9ed1d0622","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"357b4990-0035-4254-a95a-ceb9ed1d0622"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"63cd268b-fc75-42b1-8ad8-415626603283"},"task_id":"63cd268b-fc75-42b1-8ad8-415626603283","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"63cd268b-fc75-42b1-8ad8-415626603283","chunk":"{\n  \"chunk_id\": \"exec-24-1\",\n  \"command\": \"sed -n '322,440p' \\\"/Users/iv/.agents/skills/project-management/references/tracked-background-spawn.md\\\"\",\n  \"description\": \"Read CR candidate snapshot section\",\n  \"exit_code\": 0,\n  \"terminal_status\": \"completed\",\n  \"output\": \"### Change Requests, review, rework, and checkpoint\\n\\nA producer that hands off inside a managed workspace publishes a **Change\\nRequest**: a revisioned record attached to the existing Task/Bug/Story, not a new\\nboard element. It carries the branch, the base and candidate tree OIDs, a binary\\npatch resource with its SHA-256, the changed-path list, and an explicit\\n`repository_delta` of `present` or `empty`.\\n\\nThe candidate is snapshotted through a temporary alternate Git index, so tracked\\nchanges, deletions, and untracked non-ignored files are all captured **without\\ntouching the managed worktree's own index**. Every `.task-board/**` entry is then\\nrestored from the base tree, which is why a `changed_paths` list can never\\ncontain a board path — the authoritative board is the control root, and a\\nworktree's `.task-board` copy is a checkout artifact. Paths dropped by that rule\\nare recorded as `dropped_board_paths` and shown by `worktree status` and in the\\nreviewer's prompt. A non-empty list is a diagnostic, usually a `git add -A` in\\nthe child, not a failure.\\n\\nConstruction runs for **every** producer completion in a managed workspace,\\nwhether or not the run carries a delivery goal. Managed workspace activation\\nturns on role, task class, containing Story and local board alone, so an ordinary\\n`task-board spawn TASK-... --role developer --background` gets an isolated\\nworktree — and the half of the feature that makes that worktree reviewable\\nactivates on exactly the same conditions.\\n\\nIt runs **after** the spawn completion artifact guard, and on the same two facts:\\nthe board reached the run's end status, and the run attached a new or updated\\ntask-scoped outcome artifact. A run that attached nothing is reverted to its\\nproducer status by that guard and leaves no Change Request at all. A read that\\nFAILS is reported as a failure and never as \\\"did not hand off\\\".\\n\\nA workspace-claiming producer that publishes nothing says why, on the run's event\\nlog and in a board note: `handoff_unsatisfied`, with the detail — the board is\\nnot at the end status, or no task-scoped outcome artifact was attached.\\n\\nThe other two reasons are recorded nowhere, on one rule: a reason that is true of\\nevery run of its class says nothing about the run in front of you, and repeating\\nit on every completion buries the case the reporting exists for. An un-isolated\\nrun skips with `no_managed_workspace`, which is true of every un-isolated run. A\\nreviewer-archetype run skips with `not_a_producer`, which is true of every\\nreviewer run — a reviewer judges a candidate rather than publishing one, so\\nnoting it would append a line describing correct behaviour to the element's\\nprogress on every single review cycle.\\n\\n`repository_delta=empty` means the producer changed no repository file. It is an\\nattribute, never a state and never terminal: the Change Request is `ready` like\\nany other, a reviewer **is** spawned, and the injected prompt says in plain terms\\nthat nothing changed and lists the outcome resources that were produced. Deciding\\nwhether that was correct is a judgement about intent versus delivery, which is\\nwhat the reviewer archetype is for.\\n\\n**The kind is derived from the board, never declared.** A Change Request is\\n`task_delta` or `story_final`, and the second is what `worktree integrate`\\naccepts as the Story's integration unit. Publication asks the board whether\\ncompleting this leaf would also close its Story — the same question\\n`worktree checkpoint` asks to REFUSE that leaf, through the same call — so the\\nleaf one command declines is exactly the leaf the other treats as the Story's\\nunit. Every indeterminate answer refuses the publication: no board access, an\\nunreadable board, or an element the board does not contain means the question was\\nnever answered, and answering `task_delta` on a failed read would leave the Story\\npermanently unintegrable with nothing recording why.\\n\\n**The base follows the kind.** A `task_delta` is measured from the Story branch\\ntip, where earlier leaves already checkpointed. A `story_final` is measured from\\nthe workspace record's **current base OID**, initially the exact selected base\\nOID produced by upstream freshness resolution and later advanced only by a\\nsuccessful final-leaf refresh or by final publication after it proves selected\\nupstream authority, checkpoint, managed branch, and checked-out HEAD have one\\nconsistent provenance. Publication accepts either the unchanged checkpoint with\\nan uncommitted candidate in the worktree, or a branch/HEAD exactly one direct,\\nsingle-parent commit past that checkpoint when the commit tree equals the tree\\nindependently snapshotted from the selected base and present worktree. It is\\nnever recomputed through the local trunk ref: that ref may remain behind the\\nupstream that provisioning fetched, which would expand the patch to\\nalready-merged predecessor work. Any OID/tree/provenance mismatch refuses before\\nvalidation or partial Change Request persistence. The candidate tree is a full\\nsnapshot, so the recorded base is also the stable input to later re-parenting\\nwhen trunk moves again.\\n\\n**A Story has at most one unaccepted producer candidate.** The worktree is\\nStory-scoped and the snapshotter has no durable per-task path ownership model.\\nBefore a producer starts, spawn therefore scans the Story's Change Requests and\\nrefuses `change_request_sibling_producer_blocked` when another element owns a\\n`draft`, `ready`, `reviewing`, `changes_requested`, `stale`, or `conflicted`\\nrevision. The refusal names the blocking task and is checked both before\\nprovisioning and after lease admission to close the concurrent-spawn race.\\nReviewers bypass the producer gate, and the same element may start rework for\\nits own revision.\\n\\n**The producer runs the landing suite and records tree-bound evidence.** When\\n`spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` is configured, publication runs it\\nin the Story worktree and records `validation` keyed to the published candidate\\ntree. The suite runs against the WORKTREE while the evidence claims a TREE, so\\nthe candidate is recomputed afterwards and compared; a suite that moved the\\nworktree — a build that drops artifacts, a codegen step, a formatter — yields\\n**no** evidence rather than evidence keyed to a tree it never saw. A failing\\nsuite is fail-sticky at its first non-zero command and cannot publish a `ready`\\ncandidate: the immutable revision is immediately `changes_requested`, its\\nbounded transcript is attached as a task-scoped system resource, and validation\\nrecords the one-based failed command index/total plus the real exit status. The\\nproducer run follows the existing non-success/recovery-successor path with a\\nretry step naming that resource. A command that could not run at all is never\\nrecorded as a pass. Passing and explicitly empty configured suites retain the\\nready/success path. See\\n[how the evidence binds](#validation-binds-to-the-tree-that-lands).\\n\\n**Write validation commands for the managed worktree they actually run in.**\\nThe validation suite runs inside that worktree alongside gitignored scratch and\\nreviewer probes, so repository-wide filesystem walks and index comparisons can\\ninspect the wrong population. One project hit all three failures in the same\\nsuite:\\n\\n| Gate | Naive command | Observed failure |\\n| --- | --- | --- |\\n| Generated-file freshness | `go generate && git diff --exit-code -- <file>` | `git diff` compared the producer's intentionally unstaged file against the index and refused a correct candidate tree. |\\n| JSON validity | `find . -name '*.json' \\\\| xargs ...` | `find` walked 287 files including scratch, then `xargs` failed with `command line cannot be assembled, too long`. |\\n| Formatting | `test -z \\\"$(gofmt -l .)\\\"` | `gofmt` descended into `.temp/` and reported a reviewer's probe as a product formatting failure. |\\n\\nEnumerate repository content with `git ls-files -co --exclude-standard` (use\\n\",\n  \"original_output_bytes\": 7901,\n  \"original_output_tokens\": 1976,\n  \"truncated\": false\n}"}}}
